@@ -140,4 +140,4 @@ Three authorities are easy to conflate here, and they are not interchangeable:
 
 The claim this protocol makes is that the agent is **directed** to escalate and **directed** not to proceed on insufficient evidence. The claim it does not make is that PromptKit OS can prevent an agent from doing otherwise.
 
-**Discovery route (current; not validated as live behavior).** This file is not named in the protocol line of the Balanced or Lite directive — that line lists `setup`, `context-sync`, `code-quality-gate`, and `subagent-delegation`. The route to this file is **transitive**: the directive points at `protocols/setup.md`, whose *Workflows & Protocols Reference* lists `context-economy.md`, and `docs/WORKFLOW-MAP.md` also links it directly. Discovery through that route has **not** been validated with recorded live transcripts, and no automated check exercises it. Balanced/Lite discoverability and sufficiency remain **open acceptance evidence**: documenting the route is not evidence that it works.
+**Discovery route (current; guarded statically, not validated as live behavior).** This file is not named in the protocol line of the Balanced or Lite directive — that line lists `setup`, `context-sync`, `code-quality-gate`, and `subagent-delegation`. The route to this file is **transitive**: the directive points at `protocols/setup.md`, whose *Workflows & Protocols Reference* lists `context-economy.md`, and `docs/WORKFLOW-MAP.md` also links it directly. The behavioral contract suites (`scripts/tests/run-behavioral-contract-tests.sh` and its `.ps1` twin) assert that route: both directive templates name `setup` in their protocols line, `protocols/setup.md` lists `context-economy.md`, and `docs/WORKFLOW-MAP.md` links it. Those assertions guard the links, not agent behavior — discovery through the route has **not** been validated with recorded live transcripts. Balanced/Lite discoverability and sufficiency remain **open acceptance evidence**: documenting the route is not evidence that it works.
